From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 16:59:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Decode block-compressed uploads and translate mip indices
 through the clamp

Two defects on textures whose format this GPU cannot sample, where
remap_unsupported_bc() substitutes an uncompressed format in the Metal layer.

UpdateSubresource wrote raw block bytes into those textures. A block row
landed as one pixel row: full width, quarter height, striped. Creation-time
uploads already decoded; streamed ones did not, and streaming is the normal
path. The gate must be a DEVICE CAPABILITY, not a format comparison -- Texture
objects still record the compressed format, so comparing them finds the
formats equal and never fires. Skip the upload rather than fall through when
the decode buffer cannot be allocated: re-uploading the raw blocks
reintroduces the corruption only under memory pressure, so it would look
intermittent.

CopySubresourceRegion never translated mip indices at all. A clamp leaves the
D3D-visible descriptor at full mip count, so copies still arrive with LOGICAL
indices and landed on the wrong physical level. Source and destination are
translated INDEPENDENTLY, and a differing bias is not an error: physical mip
L-b has the dimensions of logical mip L, so extents and origins stay correct
and only the index shifts. Copies below a texture's own bias are dropped.

Both paths carry counters, because the failure is silent otherwise. The copy
path saw 1,860 operations during gameplay against 0 in the menu, which is why
the corruption looked like it appeared from nowhere.

